\par\par \b \uc2 Lebanese security forces fire tear gas at crowds protesting pound devaluation\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2023\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, March 22 (Reuters) - Lebanese security forces on Wednesday fired tear gas at hundreds of protesters, mostly retired members of the security forces, who had gathered near government buildings in Beirut in anger at deteriorating economic conditions.\par \par \uc2 Crowds gathered in the streets of downtown Beirut between parliament and the government serail, carrying Lebanon's tricolour or flags bearing the logos of security forces.\par \par \uc2 They were outraged at the deteriorating value of state pensions paid in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pound has lost more than 98% of its value against the U.S. dollar since 2019.\par \par \uc2 "Our kids are hungry. We're hungry," said Mohamad el-Khateeb, a 59-year-old who had served in the army for 32 years.\par \par \uc2 "We left the army with nothing. No healthcare, no welfare, our kids are out of school and prices are rising obscenely. What do you expect?" Khateeb told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Some of the men tried to cross one of the checkpoints leading to a government building, prompting security forces to fire tear gas to keep them back, according to a Reuters witness.\par \par \uc2 Protesters dashed away from white clouds emanating from locations around the serail. One soldier was seen treating a young boy who was affected by the tear gas.\par \par \uc2 "If he fires on us, he's firing on our rights and on his rights at the same time," said army veteran Ahmad Mustafa, 60.\par \par \uc2 "He's suffering just like me," he told Reuters, clutching two of the tear gas canisters fired just moments earlier.\par \par \uc2 There was no immediate statement from the Lebanese army.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's economic meltdown began in 2019 after decades of profligate spending and alleged corruption. Its onset prompted the most wide-ranging protests the country had seen in years, but they fizzled out and rallies have been sporadic since.\par \par \uc2 The country's top financial and political leaders have allowed the crisis to fester, with the Lebanese pound hitting an all-time low of 140,000 to the U.S. dollar on Tuesday before an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's army troops and members of the security forces are receiving salary support in U.S. dollars from the United States and Qatar for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Issam Abdallah, Mohamed Azakir and Ahmed Kurdi; Writing by Maya Gebeily; Editing by Bernadette Baum) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-3-22T12:25:03.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230322ej3m01wad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
