\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Lebanon's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  offers dollars amid deep financial crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2021\par 16:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)\par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, Dec 16 (Reuters) - \uc2 Lebanon's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it would sell U.S. dollars to commercial banks at the rate on its Sayrafa \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  platform, but analysts said offering more hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do little to steady the already crippled Lebanese pound.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's economy has been in freefall since 2019, when a mountain of debt and political gridlock, drove the nation into its deepest crisis since the 1975-1990 civil war.\par \par \uc2 The Lebanese pound, which was exchanged freely at 1,500 to the dollar before the crisis, has collapsed to around 25,000 on the unofficial market. On the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  platform, known as Sayrafa, Thursday's rate was 22,300.\par \par \uc2 Beirut-based financial adviser Mike Azar said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest move was "to stabilise the lira but an insolvent bank that is bleeding \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... cannot stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has no credibility."\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged into crisis, depositors - many of whom had dollar accounts - were locked out of their savings and only allowed to withdraw them in Lebanese pounds at a rate that was far below the unofficial exchange rate on the street.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said depositors withdrawing from their dollar accounts would receive Lebanese pounds at a rate of 8,000 to the dollar, an improvement on the previous rate of 3,900, but still well below the street rate or Sayrafa rate.\par \par \uc2 In Thursday's statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said depositors could use Lebanese pounds they had withdrawn to buy back dollars but at the Sayrafa rate, effectively meaning they receive a roughly 70% 'haircut' on the value of their original dollar deposit.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Lilian Wagdy and Maha el Dahan; Editing by Andrew Heavens and Edmund Blair) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-12-16T16:36:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211216ehcg038fp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
