\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Lebanon's fractious politics puts French lifeline at risk\b0\par\par\uc2 845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2020\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Sept. 22 with no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 * France has drawn up timeline to deliver reforms\par \par \uc2 * Lebanese leaders already missed cabinet deadline\par \par \uc2 * New government faces huge task to save economy\par \par \uc2 * Sectarian politics throws up roadblocks to change\par \par \uc2 By Ellen Francis\par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Lebanon's sectarian politicians have overshot one deadline they had agreed with France and missing more may put at risk a French lifeline to haul the Middle East nation out of its worst crisis since a 1975-1990 civil war.\par \par \uc2 France has drawn up a timeline for Lebanon to tackle corruption and deliver reforms to help secure billions of dollars in foreign aid to save a country drowning in debt.\par \par \uc2 But the leaders who oversaw years of wasted state spending and corruption have stumbled at the first hurdle by failing to deliver on a promise to French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  to form a new cabinet by mid-September.\par \par \uc2 Yet choosing a cabinet may prove the easy bit. Once named, the ministers have a mountain of challenges, ranging from reviving a paralysed banking industry to fixing a power sector that cannot keep the lights on in a nation of about 6 million.\par \par \uc2 Macron, who visited Beirut after a devastating Beirut port blast in August, has told politicians they could face sanctions if graft gets in the way. And Paris has repeatedly said there will be no aid without change.\par \par \uc2 WHAT PREVENTED LEBANON NAMING A GOVERNMENT QUICKLY?\par \par \uc2 Factional, sectarian politics is to blame. At the heart of the cabinet logjam has been a demand by the two main Shi'ite Muslim parties, Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its ally Amal, to pick several ministers and to keep the finance post in their hands.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry will have a vital role in drawing up plans to exit the economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister-designate Mustapha Adib, a Sunni under Lebanon's sectarian power-sharing system, had sought to shake up the leadership of ministries, some of which have been controlled by the same factions for years.\par \par \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , whose political influence has grown, and Amal view moves to shift them out of key cabinet posts as a bid to weaken their sway, politicians say.\par \par \uc2 They have a parliament majority with their Christian and other allies, although the cabinet dispute has put them at odds. President \uc2 Michel Aoun\uc2 , a Maronite Christian allied to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , has said no sect should claim any ministry.\par \par \uc2 But Washington's decision in September to impose U.S. sanctions on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's allies deepened the Shiite bloc's resolve to dig in over cabinet appointments, political sources say. Washington deems \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  a terrorist group.\par \par \uc2 Yet foreign pressure could also deliver results. Macron's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bickering leaders to agree on the prime minister-designate hours before the French president arrived in Beirut on his second visit in less than a month.\par \par \uc2 WHAT'S AT STAKE?\par \par \uc2 France has said Lebanon faces collapse if it doesn't change course. The Lebanese president has said the country is going "to hell" if doesn't name a cabinet. Many Lebanese, thousands of whom took to the streets last year to demand change, have already been plunged into poverty as the economy has crashed.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon needs cash - and fast - after defaulting on its towering sovereign debt and with its banks on their knees. The Beirut port blast, which killed almost 200 people, handed the nation with a new repair bill estimated at up to $4.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been using up dwindling foreign reserves to subsidise vital imports of wheat, fuel and medicine. Lifting subsidies, which the central has said cannot go on indefinitely, will bring more misery and may stoke tensions.\par \par \uc2 Minor episodes of sectarian unrest and factional skirmishes have accompanied the economic freefall. Further deterioration threatens more flare-ups, while security forces are paid in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is rapidly losing its value.\par \par \uc2 Donors who promised billions of dollars to help Lebanon in a 2018 Paris conference refused to hand over the cash when the country failed to deliver reforms. They have made changing course a condition for any future help.\par \par \uc2 Macron delivered a stark message in Beirut on Sept. 1: "If your political class fails, then we will not come to Lebanon's aid."\par \par \uc2 WILL THINGS GET EASIER WITH A GOVERNMENT IN PLACE?\par \par \uc2 There are big challenges ahead. France has drawn up a detailed roadmap for the new cabinet, ranging from swiftly restarting talks with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to launching tenders to start building new power stations.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, France has said the government must start to tackle endemic corruption quickly in order to secure funds at another donor conference that Paris has said it is ready to hold in the second half of October.\par \par \uc2 This means any new government faces a tight deadline. It may prove a tall order for Lebanon's fractious politicians who have already failed to name a cabinet on time. (Editing by Tom Perry and Edmund Blair) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-24T07:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200924eg9o00q05\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
