\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Lebanon: The "Pearl of the Middle East" crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 "The city's Christian quarters have been largely destroyed. People don't even have money to buy new windows or whatever it takes to rebuild their homes. They have no money, because there are no dollars in the country, everything has to be imported, and now there is no port. Basically people are abandoned," says Third Martin Keulertz, an academic in the food safety program at Beirut's \uc2 American University\uc2 , which describes the plight of the Lebanese capital, after it was hit by two explosions on Tuesday, which left at least 150 dead and more than five thousand injured. It is estimated that 12% are still unable to return to their homes.\par \par \uc2 A fire in the port of Beirut - known as the Ali Baba Cave for its corruption - blew up 2,750 tons of ammonium nitrate that were there, after a ship was confiscated in 2013. The investigations now focus on port staff and the President, \uc2 Michel Aoun\uc2 , yesterday said that he learned of the huge amount of explosives nearly three weeks before they exploded, but that he had ordered action to be taken, clarifying that he had no authority over the port facility.\par \par \uc2 In a column published by Foreign Policy magazine, analyst Oz Katerji notes that the explosion was Lebanon's Chernobyl. "It's like a Soviet disaster, it's the work of immense incompetence, endemic corruption and neglect, and its impact will spread beyond the initial explosion."\par \par \uc2 Keulertz agrees and says that what happened at the port "is a symptom of a state that has neglected its central services, not only is ammonium nitrate, but it is also garbage that is not collected, lack of electricity, lack of water supply, lack of food available to people, is a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it is basically a symptom of the country where the corrupt elite has taken all the financial resources and has taken all the financial resources and has abandoned everything else, including their core duties."\par \par \uc2 Faysal Itani, deputy director of The Center for Global Policy in Washington in the late 1990s, worked as a teenager entering port data during the digitization process. "This reflects a systemic problem with Lebanon's public institutions. They are directed and staffed according to criteria of political or sectarian affiliation, not merit. Corruption is widespread and work ethic and morals are bad. This seems to be a case of great incompetence and is on par with the way other public institutions are handled," he tells La Tercera.\par \par \uc2 In it, Heiko Wimmen line, a Lebanon-based analyst at the International Crisis Group thinks that "the networks of political influence, clientelism and corruption that political elites built over three decades have compromised accountability, due process and professional conduct at all levels."\par \par \uc2 How did Lebanon, which was considered the pearl of the Middle East, end in this crisis? The country is going through its worst moments since the long civil war (1975 to 1990). Even before the coronavirus pandemic earlier this year, Lebanon seemed to be on a road to collapse, with power outages of up to 20 hours a day, mountains of garbage in the streets and long lines at gas stations.\par \par \uc2 In March, Lebanon for the first time in its history went into default on foreign debt payments. Prime Minister Hasan Diab revealed that the country is dragging a public debt of 170% of GDP. In addition, it admitted that more than 50% of the population will soon findbelow the poverty line. In a speech on 24 April, Diab directly accused the governor of the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Riad Salame\uc2 , of the free fall of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Unemployment stands at 25%.\par \par \uc2 On October 17 last year a series of protests, known as the "revolution for hope," led to the resignation of \uc2 Saad Hariri\uc2 's government in January, and Diab assumed. His appointment was agreed by the coalition composed of the Shiite groups \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Amal, as well as the Christian and ally Free Patriotic Current, the two that occupy the most seats in Parliament after the 2018 elections. Then came the coronavirus, which has only come to worsen the economic situation of the country.\par \par \uc2 It was in this context that the explosion that destroyed the port, which is the heart of Lebanon's supplies, occurred. Itani points out that the country imports about 80% of all its essential requirements, especially food, including 90% of its wheat. About 60% of its total imports pass through the port of Beirut. "Now that the port is destroyed there is no way to get food. Of course, we have the airport. But this is more expensive and you can't bring large amounts of cereal, for example. Smaller ports, such as Tripoli in the north, now have to be used, but it's not enough. What Lebanon needs is to open its borders to Syria. They have no choice," Keulertz says.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, outrage continues to grow in the population, which on Thursday night came out to protest against the ruling elite. "It is unclear how the country can move from protests to real political change, because ruling elites are deeply entrenched and there is no organized alternative. On top of that, you have \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  as a key beneficiary of the current political system and is unlikely to accept real change," Itani says.\par \par \uc2 For Wimmen, there will be more protests, although it is not clear what dimensions they will have, because the Lebanese are very angry. "The new demonstrations could get completely out of control," he says. In the same vein, Keulertz says it is time for international \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because otherwise \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on its way to becoming a "failed state," with serious implications for the whole Middle East and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200808eg870008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
