\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves fine for now, future  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  possible - \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  head\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2017\par 15:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Finance minister asks cabinet to extend term of c.bank governor\par \par \uc2 * Lebanon \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stable for now -c.bank\par \par \uc2 * C.bank could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to guarantee monetary stability\par \par \uc2 * C.bank has no plans to raise interest rates (Adds finance minister asking for extension of c.bank head's term)\par \par \uc2 By Lisa Barrington\par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, May 15 (Reuters) - Lebanon's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were stable and did not require immediate boosting via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the bank would take measures to maintain monetary stability if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Riad Salameh\uc2  's term of office expires at the start of August and the Lebanese finance minister has asked cabinet to extend it, a Lebanese government source told Reuters on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Salameh has steered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for almost 24 years and is widely seen as a guarantor of monetary stability.\par \par \uc2 Under his leadership the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year carried out what the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  termed "unconventional" financial engineering to boost falling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 "Today we have a situation that is stable which doesn't warrant any special \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," governor \uc2 Riad Salameh\uc2  told Reuters on the sidelines of a Euromoney conference in Beirut on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "Depending on what would be necessary, we have various strategies we can implement, it is not essential that we go back to the same strategy we used in 2016."\par \par \uc2 Between June and August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchanged some of its Lebanese pound-denominated debt holdings for dollar-denominated Finance Ministry Eurobonds, while private banks transferred in dollars and were given the Eurobonds and newly issued dollar certificates of deposit in exchange.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also bought pound-denominated bonds held on local banks' books for the full principal amount plus interest up to maturity, instantly boosting their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 By mid-October reserves had risen to $41 billion from around $35 billion before the financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then they have declined again and on 30 April stood at $39.3 billion, according to the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures.\par \par \uc2 Salameh said there was currently no need for "immediate special initiatives."\par \par \uc2 GROWTH SHORTFALL\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's economy has for years been hindered by regional unrest, including the war in neighbouring Syria, and political crisis at home.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  said its GDP increased by 1 percent in 2015 and projected a similar rate for 2016, compared with a potential growth rate of 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Public debt stood at 138 percent of GDP in 2015.\par \par \uc2 Salameh said the appointment of President \uc2 Michel Aoun\uc2  after a two-and-a-half-year period without a president, and the formation of a government with \uc2 Saad al-Hariri\uc2  as prime minister late last year has created a basis for better growth, Salameh said.\par \par \uc2 But Lebanon is on the brink of crisis again with its politicians at odds over an election law at the heart of the nation's sectarian system, threatening to leave the country without a parliament for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Parliament\uc2  's term expires on June 20 and without a compromise Lebanon faces what one minister has called the most serious political crisis since the end of its 1975-90 civil war.\par \par \uc2 Salameh said while the \uc2 IMF\uc2  was predicting growth of 2-3 percent for 2017, the impact of the political situation was as yet unclear.\par \par \uc2 Salameh has been in his post since 1993 and banking figures have called for his term of office to be extended past August to send a message of stability to markets.\par \par \uc2 He told Reuters the government had not yet decided if his term was to be extended, but a government source said it was likely.\par \par \uc2 "The finance minister (Ali Hassan Khalil) sent a letter to the council of ministers asking for the extension of the term of the governor of Banque du Liban by six years," the source said, speaking on condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 "The atmosphere is headed towards approval of the appointment, but it is not certain it will happen this week."\par \par \uc2 Salameh said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not plan to change rates.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a mandate from the government to keep stability for the Lebanese pound and there is a necessity to keep interest rates stable," he told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Lisa Barrington, additional reporting by Laila Bassam; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-5-15T14:27:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170515ed5f009yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
