\par\par \b \uc2 MIDEAST DEBT-Loyal banks, diaspora help Lebanon ride out region's turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2013\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Capital inflows to Lebanon falling sharply\par \par \uc2 * But remittances staying strong\par \par \uc2 * This allows banks to continue buying government debt\par \par \uc2 * Less pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg than in past crises\par \par \uc2 * Sovereign bonds have outperformed this year\par \par \uc2 By Andrew Torchia and Mirna Sleiman\par \par \uc2 DUBAI, Aug 1 (Reuters) - Lebanon's politics are descending into sectarian conflict and its economy is starved of investment. But its sovereign bonds are steady, foreign reserves are holding up and there is no sign of serious pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That striking contrast suggests the country may avoid the economic crisis which has engulfed other nations during the Arab Spring uprisings, even though it is suffering increasing damage from the civil war in neighbouring Syria.\par \par \uc2 An overseas diaspora of around 14 million people, more than three times the size of Lebanon's domestic population of about 4 million, continues to send billions of dollars back to the country each year.\par \par \uc2 This is swelling bank deposits and allowing banks to keep buying government debt, which means the government can boost spending to try to ease social tensions - and maintain a minimum level of political stability needed to attract more remittances.\par \par \uc2 It is a three-pronged arrangement based on mutual need that has sustained Lebanon during repeated political crises since the end of its civil war in 1990, and which is so far working well in the current instability, bankers and economists say.\par \par \uc2 "The situation is not ideal but people aren't panicking," said Nassib Ghobril, chief economist at the \uc2 Byblos Bank\uc2  Group in Beirut. "The country has coped with similar situations before."\par \par \uc2 REDUCED INFLOWS\par \par \uc2 By triggering a flare-up of sectarian tensions in Lebanon, the Syrian civil war is taking a heavy toll on the Lebanese economy.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister-designate Tammam Salam has been unable to form a cabinet since March, when his predecessor quit, and     parliamentary elections have been delayed until November 2014. Rival militias and the army have been battling in the coastal cities of Sidon and Tripoli.\par \par \uc2 This has slashed inflows of portfolio and direct investment; tourism revenues have also tumbled as wealthy Gulf states and other countries have issued travel warnings to their citizens because of poor security.\par \par \uc2 The result has been a sharp reduction of capital flows into Lebanon. Net private capital inflows shrank to $2.4 billion last year from a peak of $12 billion in 2009, and are expected to drop further to just $1.6 billion in 2013, according to the Institute of International Finance, a global banking body.\par \par \uc2 For an economy with a gross domestic product of about $45 billion, that is a big blow. From levels around 8 percent in 2007-2010, economic growth slipped to just 1.3 percent last year, and it is expected to be close to that level this year.\par \par \uc2 But inflows of remittances from Lebanese abroad have been stable. The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  estimates they totalled $7.5 billion last year, flat from 2011, and bankers in Beirut say the latest political turmoil has not affected them significantly.\par \par \uc2 This has allowed deposits at Lebanese commercial banks to continue growing; combined deposits of private sector residents and non-residents at commercial banks climbed to 182.6 trillion Lebanese pounds ($121 billion) in May from 168.3 trillion pounds a year earlier, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 Philippe El Hajj, deputy general manager at \uc2 Fransabank\uc2  in Beirut, estimated banking system deposits grew 3 percent in the first half of 2013 and predicted a 5-6 percent increase in 2013.\par \par \uc2 "This is mainly driven by Lebanese expats and by the accumulated interest on deposits. I don't see why remittances to Lebanon should decrease - those helping their families at home won't stop and will find many ways to continue supporting them."\par \par \uc2 Rising deposits have in turn permitted Lebanese banks to continue buying their government's debt, and to buy into any selling by foreign investors, keeping prices of the country's bonds remarkably stable.\par \par \uc2 Bid at 6.05 percent, the yield on Lebanon's $650 million bond maturing in 2019 is up just 40 basis points since mid-May, outperforming many emerging market bonds, where yields have jumped 100 bps or more because of concern about rising \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields.\par \par \uc2 One potential threat to Lebanese remittances is a political backlash against Shi'ism in the Sunni Gulf; Gulf Arab states are punishing \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria by expelling Lebanese expatriates linked to the group, and are keen to block any illicit fund flows to Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 But it is not clear that the number of people affected will be nearly large enough to cut the global amount of remittances. Meanwhile, other events abroad have helped Lebanon; the debt crisis in Cyprus caused Lebanese to bring back hundreds of millions of dollars from that country this year, bankers said.\par \par \uc2 CRISES\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's dependence on remittances is not without costs. For example, its banks keep their interest rates about 3 percentage points higher than U.S. rates in order to attract deposits, even though the Lebanese pound is pegged to the U.S. dollar. This slows lending and economic growth.\par \par \uc2 But as long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg holds, sustaining the diaspora's confidence that their remittances will not lose value, the system is a robust one.\par \par \uc2 So far, in contrast to some other crises in the past two decades, the current political instability has not appeared to threaten a run on the pound. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's combined holdings of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold edged up to $44.4 billion in May from $44.0 billion a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Ghobril said the pound came under pressure three times in recent years. The assassination of former prime minister Rafik al-Hariri in 2005 caused an outflow of bank deposits worth 5 percent of GDP over several weeks; Israel's attack on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in 2006 prompted a 3 percent outflow; and the 2011 fall of Saad al-Hariri's government led to an outflow of under 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Since the peg survived those incidents, a bigger political shock would be needed to even raise the possibility of it being dislodged in future, he said.\par \par \uc2 Another vulnerability is the risk that state debt, now about 140 percent of GDP, could expand beyond the capacity of the banks to fund it. The current disarray in the government could worsen the problem by blocking efforts to control spending; the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  expects the budget deficit to rise to 9.7 percent of GDP this year from 9.0 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 But with commercial bank deposits at roughly twice the level of government debt, there appears to be little risk of banks losing their capacity to buy bonds for the foreseeable future - as long as deposits do not fall sharply.\par \par \uc2 "The pound's peg to the dollar survived throughout the past 30 years despite a 15-year civil war, conflicts with Syria and multiple Israeli wars," said a source close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining to be named because of the sensitivity of the subject.\par \par \uc2 "Why would that change now? It's quite impossible."\par \par \uc2 LEBANON-CRISIS/ (MIDEAST DEBT) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | GBN | GFN | ABX | BNX | FUN | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130801e981000uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
