\par\par \b \uc2 Syrian crackdown on protest seen scaring investors\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Foreign companies reluctant to commit capital-businessman\par \par \uc2 * Ample foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Khaled Yacoub Oweis\par \par \uc2 DAMASCUS, March 23 (Reuters) - The Syrian pound has fallen 4 percent and Damascus stock prices have tumbled since protests against Baathist rule erupted last week, threatening efforts to attract foreign investment to improve Syria's infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The government had hoped to use the bourse to raise capital as it opens sectors such as electricity generation and transport to foreign investors, ending decades of state control.\par \par \uc2 But a violent crackdown on nearly a week of protests in Deraa and other southern towns, in which security forces have killed 10 people, has been a severe setback for the drive to attract badly needed foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday alone, security forces killed six people in an attack on a mosque in Deraa, site of unprecedented protests against President Bashar al-Assad's Baathist rule and demands for an end to corruption.\par \par \uc2 A Syrian businessman who has met Western companies to discuss forming joint ventures to bid for government projects said the projects could well be viable, but the rising political risk had prevented the talks from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 "There is reluctance to put big money into Syria now," he said, referring to a billion dollar metro line project and the planned expansion of a highway to Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment fell to $1.4 billion in 2009 from $2.4 billion in 2008, according to the World Bank --- a fraction of the $85 billion the government says it needs to overhaul the transport system, power, health and communication network.\par \par \uc2 "Officials are talking about technicalities of projects as if political discontent does not exist," another businessman said. "They have not figured out that an open political system and rule of law are powerful investment magnets."\par \par \uc2 On the 20-company Damascus Stock Exchange, which opened two years ago, every stock that has traded since the protests erupted in Deraa on Friday fell by the 3 percent limit. The main market index has fallen 14 percent from its Jan. 26 2011 peak.\par \par \uc2 Even before the latest protests, foreigners were deterred from buying Syrian shares by extensive security checks, and by the U.S. sanctions imposed on Syria in 2004 for its support for militant groups, a bourse official said.\par \par \uc2 "The risk of holding the Syrian pound has gone up. There is demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and people are reluctant to part with their dollars," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Damascus.\par \par \uc2 The Syrian pound, which is not fully convertible -- a legacy of Soviet-style economic policies -- was trading at 48.75 to the dollar at exchange dealers on Wednesday, though the official rate remained stable at 47 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Syria's economy underwent heavy nationalisation after the Baath Party took power in 1963. Assad, who succeeded his late father Hafez al-Assad in 2000, kept the authoritarian system intact, including the imposition of emergency laws, but lifted some bans on trading and private enterprise.\par \par \uc2 Privately held banks were allowed to operate in Syria eight years ago, and there are now 14 -- all subsidiaries of Arab banks -- but the sector is still far smaller than that of laissez faire \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  next door.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market in 2005, when the assassination of Lebanese statesman Rafik al-Hariri in Beirut raised international pressure on Syria and pushed the pound down to 59 to the dollar from 46, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market.\par \par \uc2 There was no immediate comment from the monetary authorities on the falling exchange rate, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said that Syria's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position is strong, with billions of dollars in liquid reserves.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Tim Pearce)\par \par \uc2 SYRIA-PROTESTS/BUSINESS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110323e73n0011v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
