\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Lebanon\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yara Bayoumy   \par \uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2010\par 16:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Expectations that Hezbollah members may be indicted over the 2005 killing of former prime minister Rafik al-Hariri have raised fears of a political confrontation that could plunge Lebanon into turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Renewed military conflict with Israel -- which fought a month-long war with Hezbollah in 2006 -- is also a possibility after Israel accused Syria earlier this year of sending long-range Scud missiles to Hezbollah. The political tensions overshadow strong economic growth and an inflow of funds to Lebanon in the last three years which bucked regional and global trends during the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Following are key Lebanon political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 THE HARIRI TRIBUNAL\par \par \uc2 Hezbollah leader Sayyed Hassan Nasrallah has denounced the U.N.-backed tribunal which is investigating Hariri's killing as an "Israeli project" and said he will reject any accusations against Hezbollah members.\par \par \uc2 Shi'ite Hezbollah, which denies any role in the killing, has called on Sunni Prime Minister Saad al-Hariri, Rafik's son, to repudiate the tribunal before it issues indictments.\par \par \uc2 The timetable for the indictments has been slipping but diplomats now expect them early next year.\par \par \uc2 Saad al-Hariri and members of his "March 14" coalition have insisted he will never disavow a process on which he has staked his political credibility and the main platform on which he won two successive elections.\par \par \uc2 If the two sides fail to resolve their differences then their fragile unity government will come under pressure. Hezbollah may threaten to resign from the cabinet as it did in 2006, marking the start of months of political turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Politicians have said the country also risks sinking into sectarian conflict similar to the crisis in May, 2008, when Hezbollah supporters briefly took over mainly Sunni Muslim west Beirut and more than 80 people were killed in street clashes.\par \par \uc2 If the Western- and Saudi-backed Hariri does repudiate the tribunal, he risks losing his credibility among his Sunni power base and may be forced to resign.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO LOOK OUT FOR:\par \par \uc2 - Regional efforts to ease the tension. The leaders of Syria, which backs Hezbollah, and Saudi Arabia made a symbolic joint visit to Beirut in July to try to calm the situation.\par \par \uc2 Any upset in the Syrian-Saudi relationship would have potentially damaging repercussions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - Pressure on the Lebanese pound. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month for the first time in two years to support the pound after political tensions pushed some traders to sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Riad Salameh said last week he is committed to maintaining the pound's trading range of 1,501 to 1,514 pounds to the dollar, and that he would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the pound fell to 1,514.\par \par \uc2 ANOTHER REGIONAL WAR?\par \par \uc2 Lebanon could be dragged into conflict with Israel, either through direct confrontation or as part of a wider war. Possible triggers include:\par \par \uc2 - A border incident which escalates into war. Israel launched its inconclusive 34-day war with Hezbollah in 2006 after a cross-border raid by Hezbollah to capture Israeli soldiers.\par \par \uc2 In August, a senior Israeli officer, two Lebanese soldiers and a Lebanese journalist were killed in a cross-border skirmish which broke out after the Israeli army used a crane to trim the branches of a tree near the border.\par \par \uc2 - Israel strikes against Hezbollah targets in Lebanon or Syria. As well as accusing Syria of providing Scud missiles to Hezbollah, Israel has also said Hezbollah is stockpiling weapons in southern Lebanon, in violation of a U.N. Security Council Resolution which ended the 2006 conflict.\par \par \uc2 - Hezbollah, which is backed by Iran, could support an Iranian military response if Israel or the United States strike Iran's nuclear programme.\par \par \uc2 "I have come to say that the Iranian nation ...will stand with you ... until the end," Iran's President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad told a rally in south Lebanon last month.\par \par \uc2 SUSTAINABILITY OF ECONOMIC GROWTH\par \par \uc2 Lebanon bounced back quickly from the 2006 Israeli-Hezbollah war and has enjoyed an average 8 percent growth for the last three years, driven by strong consumer confidence in what is mostly a service-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 Tourism numbers have soared, property prices boomed and banks recorded double-digit profit margins.\par \par \uc2 But Lebanon's run-down road, water, electricity and communications networks discourage many investors. The economy minister says at least $20 billion is needed to upgrade infrastructure or growth will "dwindle and vanish".\par \par \uc2 Lebanese bankers also say that while the economy can withstand the current domestic political deadlock, full-blown conflict would inevitably wreak great damage.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TO LOOK OUT FOR:\par \par \uc2 - Will the investment take place? The 2010 and 2011 budgets, earmarking $4 billion of infrastructure investment, have yet to be passed by parliament due to political bickering.\par \par \uc2 Economists say failure to agree economic reform will curb long term growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 - Economists are also worried that increased fiscal spending is adding to a massive public debt, estimated at around $51 billion, making Lebanon one of the most indebted countries in the world. The 2010 budget envisages $13 billion for spending.\par \par \uc2 - If sectarian troubles escalate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has significant reserves to maintain the stability of the Lebanese pound. Governor Riad Salameh said last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has $31 billion in liquid foreign assets and nearly $12 billion in gold reserves.\par \par \uc2 LEBANON-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101101e6b1001f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
