\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Summit-Lebanon inflows ease, lending strong -Audi CFO\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2010\par 17:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters Middle East Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/MiddleEastInvestment10?pid=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/MiddleEastInvestment10?pid=500}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Inflows down 8 pct but bank loans to private sector jump\par \par \uc2 * Says Lebanon could afford 40 pct fall in capital inflows\par \par \uc2 * Only "minority" selling Lebanese pound\par \par \uc2 By Dominic Evans BEIRUT, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Capital inflows into Lebanon fell around 8 percent in the first eight months of the year to around $11.9 billion, a senior banker said on Monday, but current levels remain sufficient to support strong domestic growth. Freddie Baz, chief financial officer for Bank Audi told the Reuters Middle East Investment Summit that Lebanon had seen cumulative inflows of $60 billion since the start of 2007.\par \par \uc2 Some of that money came from Lebanese residents and expatriates moving money back to Lebanon's banks, seen as relatively safe havens during the global economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 The inflows helped support bank loans for Lebanon's private sector and sustain recent GDP growth of around 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We indeed witnessed in the first eight months of 2010 an 8 percent decrease in inflows," Baz said, citing Bank Audi figures extrapolated from official balance of payment and trade balance figures.\par \par \uc2 But he said Lebanon, which last year saw $20.6 billion of inflows, could afford a 40 percent drop in that figure without affecting liquidity or the ability of domestic banks to supply the required financing for the domestic economy to ensure a 5 to 6 percent real growth in GDP.\par \par \uc2 Loans to the private sector actually increased in the first eight months of 2010 by $4.9 billion, more than double the increase in the corresponding period of last year, he said.\par \par \uc2 Baz said remittances from Lebanese citizens abroad accounted for a little over 40 percent of the inflows, with foreign direct investment (FDI) another 40 percent and "unilateral transfers" from Lebanon's huge overseas diaspora accounting for the rest. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ For other news on Reuters Summits, please double click on [RSUM] ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 POLITICAL TENSIONS\par \par \uc2 "The most stable parts (of the inflows) are remittances and the unilateral transfers," he said. "The buffer which increases or decreases according to the political environment is FDI."\par \par \uc2 Tensions have been rising in Lebanon since media reports that indictments are expected against members of the militant Shi'ite group Hezbollah for alleged involvement in the 2005 assassination of former prime minister Rafik al-Hariri.\par \par \uc2 Bankers say \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks ago for the first time in two years to buy Lebanese pounds, after fears that the political row could escalate pushed the dollar to the outer limits of its exchange rate against the pound.\par \par \uc2 Baz said only a "very little minority" had been selling the pound, and the country's economy was sound -- pointing to a reduction in foreign debt, a build-up of foreign reserves and cumulative growth of 25 percent over the last three years.\par \par \uc2 "There has been some buying of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... but it doesn't represent a trend in the system," he said.\par \par \uc2 "The Lebanese pound is still the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of saving and there are a lot of people who have shifted their holdings into Lebanese pounds, who are quite happy and comfortable."\par \par \uc2 He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should consider easing the peg on the Lebanese pound, which is set at around 1,507.5 pounds to the dollar and trades at between 1,501 and 1,514 pounds.\par \par \uc2 He said a 5 percent "corridor" would help the Lebanese get used to "a certain volatility in the exchange rate", and prevent an exaggerated response to a minor fluctuation. (Editing by Greg Mahlich)\par \par \uc2 MIDEAST-SUMMIT/LEBANON-FUNDS|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|MD|GFN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101018e6ai0015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
