\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Saudi gives battered Lebanon economy $1.5 bln cushion  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Souhail Karam  \par \uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2006\par 16:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details throughout)  \par \par \uc2 RIYADH, July 25 (Reuters) - Saudi Arabia's King Abdullah has ordered the transfer of $1 billion to Lebanon's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help it support an economy battered by an Israeli military offensive.  \par \par \uc2 King Abdullah ordered a separate $500 million donation earlier on Tuesday to rebuild Lebanon.  \par \par \uc2 State television said on Tuesday the money would be "deposited" with the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , without specifying whether it would be a loan or a grant. Saudi \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials could not be reached for comment.  \par \par \uc2 Pressure on the Lebanese pound has risen during two weeks of Israeli air strikes that began after Lebanon's Hizbollah guerrillas captured two Israeli soldiers in a cross-border attack.  \par \par \uc2 Israel's offensive has so far claimed almost 400 lives in Lebanon and destroyed infrastructure in the small Arab state.  \par \par \uc2 "This will relieve pressure on the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the credit worthiness of the country will be boosted," said Haissam Arabi of investment bank Shuaa Capital in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "There is more than $2 billion worth of infrastructure damage in Lebanon and this shows that Lebanon has its allies in Saudi," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia, the world's largest oil exporter, has been a major political and economic sponsor of Lebanon since the 1975-1990 civil war was ended by an agreement made in the Saudi city of Taif.  \par \par \uc2 A Western diplomat in Riyadh said the move was a sign of a tussle for influence in Lebanon between Iran, which backs Hizbollah, and Arab states led by Saudi Arabia.  \par \par \uc2 "They are saying 'we all have to get in there before the Iranians do'. It's going to be a battle between Iranians propping up Hizbollah and Gulf Arabs propping up the Lebanese government," he said.  \par \par \uc2 PROLONGED SIEGE  \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia has played a key role in supporting the Lebanese pound since 1990, and billionaire Saudi Prince Alwaleed bin Talal is one of Lebanon's major foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon's late Prime Minister Rafik Hariri, who led reconstruction efforts, was also a Saudi national.  \par \par \uc2 With fears mounting of a prolonged siege after Israel blockaded Lebanon from sea, air and land, Lebanese have been hoarding dollars, creating a shortage of banknotes.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon is a highly dollarised economy, with most shops as ready to accept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they are the Lebanese pound. People, hurt by a financial collapse during the civil war, are accustomed to holding hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Arabi said the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had about $14 billion of reserves and was well positioned to defend the pound. "There is some pressure on the pound and demand for dollars," he said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  depends heavily on foreign direct investment, which amounted to about 10 percent of gross domestic product in 2005, to finance a current account deficit that hit nearly 13 percent of GDP last year.  \par \par \uc2 Gulf Arab investors have poured money into Lebanese real estate in recent years, while small but growing flows of petrodollars helped push Lebanon's stock market up about 10 percent this year before the fighting erupted.  \par \par \uc2 A newspaper in the United Arab Emirate reported on Tuesday that UAE companies had more than $2.7 billion invested in Lebanon.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Will Rasmussen and Andrew Hammond)  \par \par \uc2 MIDEAST-SAUDI-LEBANON (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|MD|GFN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|BNX|RNP|DNP|PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060725e27p001gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
