\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Political crisis hits Lebanon's economy-c.bank  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Khaled Yacoub Oweis and Lin Noueihed  \par \uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2005\par 15:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, June 16 (Reuters) - Lebanon has slashed its 2005 growth forecast to two percent after the killing of former prime minister Rafik al-Hariri shook the country's financial markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Riad Salameh said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon had predicted growth of six percent for this year before the Feb. 14 killing threw the country into its worst political crisis since the 1975-1990 civil war, compared to five percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "The events in the two months after Hariri's assassination affected growth. Before, we had expected more than six percent," he told Reuters on the sidelines of the Arab Economic Forum in Beirut.  \par \par \uc2 "The two percent figure is for the whole year, taking into account the negative growth in these two months. But we see that there is optimism and there is interest in Lebanon including more from the West than before."  \par \par \uc2 Despite that optimism, Salameh struck a cautious note about Lebanon's longer term prospects for economic recovery, urging the government to forge ahead with financial reforms to curtail a growing public debt.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon's public debt stands at around $36 billion, of which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds $11 billion, he said.  \par \par \uc2 That debt has been accumulated since the war, mostly paying salaries in a public sector filled with political appointees. Debt servicing now consumes the bulk of government revenues.  \par \par \uc2 Prime minister for much of the postwar period, Hariri had long promised reforms to international lenders, but progress was paralysed by power struggles in Lebanon's sectarian political system.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the debt continues to grow. Lebanon issued a $500 million eurobond on Wednesday to finance the state.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy aims to stabilise prices, especially exchange rates. We will continue with this policy because it has allowed us to survive crises and overcome inflationary pressures," he said, forecasting four percent inflation for the year, similar to 2004 levels.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1,507.5 to the pound after Hariri's death unnerved investors, prompting them to switch to the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 The dollarisation rate of deposits has since fallen from 80 percent to 76 percent, said the former Merrill Lynch executive credited with stabilising a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  virtually in freefall when he took the helm of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1993.  \par \par \uc2 Salameh's other main focus is the health of the banking sector, which holds most of the government's debt and whose deposits, estimated at around $58 billion at the end of last year, far exceed the country's entire gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 Private bank deposits have fallen three percent so far this year, but there had been no significant capital flight despite Hariri's killing, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon's business community had feared a financial meltdown after the death of Hariri, whose international connections had helped Lebanon attract financial assistance.  \par \par \uc2 "Lebanon has gone through a tough period which had its impact on economic growth and financial confidence," Salameh said. "We are now witnessing a return to economic and investment activity."  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-LEBANON (INTERVIEW, PICTURE)|