\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Lebanese pound ends unchanged, cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lin Noueihed  \par \uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 17:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, March 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pound came under renewed pressure on Tuesday after the surprise resignation of the government under popular pressure unsettled investors already jittery over the killing of an ex-prime minister.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to maintain the pound at the weakest end of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band of 1,501-1,514 against the dollar. But it closed unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of demand against the dollar today. People are worried that the government's resignation has left a political vacuum," said one Lebanese trader.  \par \par \uc2 "They have to begin consultations for a new government that is neutral and acceptable quickly. If a solution is on the way it will be all right. If not, it is a problem."  \par \par \uc2 Lebanese officials began looking for a new premier after the Syrian-backed government resigned on Monday following two weeks of protests.  \par \par \uc2 But it was not clear how long it would take to form a new cabinet and whether the lineup would calm a market already shaken by the killing of Rafik al-Hariri, whom Lebanon's business community considered the country's best hope for economic revival.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were reluctant to say how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent propping up the pound. It spent over $800 million in total in the first three sessions after Hariri's death, they said, but pressure quickly eased.  \par \par \uc2 One trader estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent $400-$500 million, about the same as the first day after Hariri's death, but others said the pressure was lower. Dollar demand also tends to rise at month-end in Lebanon.  \par \par \uc2 "There was demand but it is not all about the government," said a trader. "There is the end of month factor. Many accounts are blocked until the end of the month, so it is usually busier."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised after Hariri's assassination to defend the pound and keep interest rates unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has enough gross \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - $11.4 billion in mid-February - to keep the pound stable for the time being.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon's commercial banks, which hold much of the country's public debt equivalent to $33 billion, are also highly liquid, their assets far exceeding gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 But some fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to raise interest rates should pressure be prolonged.  \par \par \uc2 "The cabinet resignation may be a good thing but the market does not like uncertainty. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has enough reserves but they are not infinite," said another trader at a large Lebanese bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Either the risk has to fall or the interest rate has to go up. Things need to happen quickly with this new government."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050301e13100b1o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
