\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon \highlight\uc2  pound steady below  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  band.  \b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2004\par 16:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, April 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pound hovered just below the bottom end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in the week ending Friday as banks who bought special CDs on a forward deal kept dollar demand up, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was trading at 1,514-1,516 pounds to the dollar in interbank trade, just outside the 1,501-1,514 band, as banks bought dollars to pay for special high-yielding certificates of deposit that require banks to sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had offered dollars but mainly to satisfy commercial demand, significant in a country that relies heavily on imports.  \par \par \uc2 "With reserves this high and the government committed to defending the pound, there is no pressure," said one dealer. "These are just the technical effects of the CDs." The limited offer officially ended months ago but traders said some banks had reached special arrangements to buy up the CDs, which offered yields of 12 percent compared to nine percent usually, on a forward deal.  \par \par \uc2 The CDs have helped boost foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that had slipped over the past two years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended the pound, which came under pressure as fears mounted over the government's ability to service its $30 billion debt.  \par \par \uc2 The pound had lifted after a 2002 debt aid summit to ease Lebanon's servicing burden briefly raised market confidence.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since slipped as promised reforms, including the long-awaited privatisation of the mobile phone sector, have been delayed by political deadlock.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were closed Monday and Tuesday for Easter.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040416e04g001x9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
