\par\par \b \uc2 Lebanon's pound steady outside band on CD option.  \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2003\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIRUT, Nov 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pound was steady outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band this week as banks who bought special CDs on a forward deal boosted dollar demand, traders said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The pound hovered around 1,516-1,517 to dollar, just outside the 1,501-1,514 band, as banks bought dollars to pay for the special high-yielding certificates of deposit that require banks to sell dollars to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The limited offer officially ended last month but traders said some banks had reached arrangements with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy up the CDs, which offered yields of over 12 percent compared to nine usually, on a forward deal.  \par \par \uc2 "The banks will probably be buying dollars to give to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the next three or four months for these CDs," one trader said. "This is an artificial level for the pound. It is not because people are giving it up."  \par \par \uc2 The CDs helped boost foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that had slipped over the past two years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended the pound, which came under pressure as fears mounted over the government's ability to service its $30 billion debt.  \par \par \uc2 Lebanon stopped issuing the special CDs last month and resumed treasury bill auctions, which it had suspended for nine months after receiving about $4 billion soft loans from international lenders to help ease its debt service last year.  \par \par \uc2 The pound strengthened after that debt aid summit but has since slipped as the government has failed to push ahead with long-delayed privatisations pledged to lenders at the time. ($1=1,507.5 Lebanese pounds).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031121dzbl003nf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
