\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese pound firms as Paris lender meet nears. \b0\par\par\uc2 290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2002\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The Lebanese pound strengthened against the dollar in the week ending Friday in the run-up to a lenders' conference that Lebanon hopes will garner up to $5 billion in low-cost loans, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said the pound was trading around 1,513 pounds to the dollar, near the weaker end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1,501-1,514 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  will seek the long-term loans at a Paris meeting scheduled for November 23 that it hopes will help reduce the interest burden on its $29 billion public debt, expected to hit 173 percent of gross domestic product by year-end. \par \par \uc2  This second Paris meeting will follow a previous session that generated pledges of about 500 million euros of assistance. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody is waiting. They are thinking it is too good to be true," one dealer said. "After Paris 2 happens, the (dollar) offers will be bigger. I think we will go toward the lower end in the band, but it will take time." \par \par \uc2  Fears about the country's ability to service the debt have put pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last two years, often forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend it. \par \par \uc2  But traders said the bank was buying hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  all week, shoring up its reserves as a rise in confidence boosted the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reduced demand for dollars. The interbank rate was steady at eight percent. \par \par \uc2  Lebanon also hopes an austere 2003 draft budget, as well as revenue from value-added tax, broader income tax and the sale of mobile phone licences and a stake in the electricity monopoly will help ease the debt burden. \par \par \uc2  ($1=1,507.5 Lebanese pounds). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021115dybf00lgo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
