\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Byblos says agrees to buy ABN in Lebanon. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Cynthia Johnston \par \uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2002\par 19:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Nov 9 (Reuters) - Lebanon's Byblos Bank has agreed to buy out the local \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Dutch bank ABN AMRO in a $36 million cash deal, Byblos chairman Francois Bassil said on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "There is some fine tuning on the contract, but we agreed on everything," Bassil told Reuters. "We are on the eve of signing." \par \par \uc2  He said Byblos would take over banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for ABN AMRO in Lebanon pending approval of the deal by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that ABN AMRO had six branches in Lebanon with around $600 million in deposits and $300 million in loans. \par \par \uc2  Bassil said the deal after several months of negotiations would expand Byblos' product base and portfolio in a market where "good assets are not easy to get". \par \par \uc2  "ABN AMRO is very well established in Lebanon with high quality personnel and risk portfolio," he said. \par \par \uc2  Faced with a sluggish economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to force \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's more than 60 banks to merge or raise their capital adequacy levels. \par \par \uc2  Bassil said that Byblos, with about $400 million in total equity, could easily finance the deal. But he added that Byblos was also planning to increase its capital by up to $150 million through the issue of preferred shares. \par \par \uc2  "We are pre-launching the issuing of $50 to $150 million in preferred shares," he said, adding the issue was also subject to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  approval. \par \par \uc2  He said he expected the shares to be issued in December or early January. \par \par \uc2  SEEKING MORE ACQUISITIONS \par \par \uc2  Asked if Byblos was still looking for more acquisitions, Bassil said: "Yes, we are looking for others." \par \par \uc2  He said the bank was currently in talks with a medium-sized bank in the region but "outside of Lebanon". \par \par \uc2  "We are in negotiations," he said, adding that he expected talks to take several more months to complete. \par \par \uc2  He added that Byblos had not yet decided whether it was interested in actually acquiring the other bank or doing a joint venture. He declined to give further details. \par \par \uc2  "We haven't fixed the way to do it. We are still in discussions," he said. "We are not in a hurry because we have to digest what we have now. But this is digestible easily, this one." \par \par \uc2  In addition to ABN AMRO, Byblos acquired investment bank ING Barings' Lebanon assets last year for an undisclosed amount. It also bought Wedge Bank in a deal market sources estimated was worth $23 million. \par \par \uc2  Byblos posted close to flat net income last month of 48.7 billion Lebanese pounds ($32.3 million) for the first nine months of 2002. \par \par \uc2  The profitability of Lebanese banks, which finance most of the country's $29 billion public debt, has been slowing as the economy has stagnated. \par \par \uc2  Many banks are adopting cautious lending and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions amid concern over state finances, to which they have significant exposure through treasury bill purchases. ($1=1,507.5 Lebanese pounds). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021109dyb900prt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
