\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese pound steady ahead of Francophone meet. \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2002\par 13:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Oct 11 (Reuters) - The Lebanese pound traded steady in the week ending Friday ahead of a Francophone conference dealers say they hope will pave the way for a donor meeting that could bring Lebanon financial support. \par \par \uc2  They said they hoped the conference would give Lebanese Prime Minister Rafik al-Hariri and French President Jacques Chirac the opportunity to work out the details of the Paris meeting, expected later this year. \par \par \uc2  Lebanon is hoping the Paris meeting of potential donors will help it borrow more cheaply and rein in a mounting public debt. \par \par \uc2  "It (financial support) is on the way," one dealer said. "During the Francophonie, Hariri and Chirac will have a clear idea for Paris 2." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the pound was trading around 1,513-1,514 pounds to the dollar, at the weaker end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1,501-1,514 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was selling small amounts of dollars to support the pound amid slightly increased dollar demand, but one dealer called the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "minimal". \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under increasing pressure as concern has mounted in recent years over the government's ability to handle the country's public debt, expected to hit 178 percent of gross domestic product by year-end. \par \par \uc2  The government is hoping that planned sales of state assets such as mobile licences, as well as tighter public finances, will help it handle a $29 billion public debt. \par \par \uc2  The government is also counting on Gulf Arabs to direct their tourism and investments to Lebanon after the September 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  Pressure on the pound fell in May after foreign investors, believed to include Malaysia and Gulf Arab states, bought over $500 million of Lebanese Eurobonds to raise the level of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021011dyab00mv3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
