\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanon cbank says completes $3 bln debt swap. \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2002\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Aug 21 (Reuters) - Lebanon's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday that it had swapped 4.5 trillion Lebanese pounds ($2.985 billion) of 2002 paper for debt maturing in 2004, the most recent in a series of swaps to manage a hefty public debt. \par \par \uc2  Lebanon is struggling to service a $29 billion debt run up in reconstruction from its 1975-1990 civil war which is expected to reach 178 percent of GDP by the end of the year, and has placed heavy pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last two years. \par \par \uc2  The country is converting high-yielding local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt into relatively cheap \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated paper, and in May the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had managed to sell $950 million of its 2005 Lebanon Eurobond holding. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Riad Salameh said the swaps had been made possible by improved sentiment in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, where the bank has often had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the Lebanese pound. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  is counting on revenue from state asset sales and a new value-added tax, as well as tighter spending, to help it rein in the debt and eventually be able to borrow more cheaply on international capital markets. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020821dy8l00pxp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
