\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanon says to seek long-term loans at aid talks. \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2002\par 20:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, July 3 (Reuters) - Lebanese Prime Minister Rafik al-Hariri said on Wednesday he hoped to obtain longer-term loans with lower interest rates at a conference of rich nations in Paris later this year. \par \par \uc2  "We hope to get from Paris financial means to obtain loans to replace the current short-term loans...with longer term loans of 10 to 15 years with lower interest," he told a banking conference on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  France has said it will host the meeting to discuss lending financial support to Lebanon, which has public debt of around $28 billion, more than 165 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Much of the debt was run up during the decade after the 1975-1990 civil war due to the heavy cost of rebuilding the shattered capital Beirut. \par \par \uc2  Hariri has said he hoped the meeting could take place before the end of the year. Leading Gulf states, the members of the Group of Eight industrialised nations, the European Union and leading international organisations would take part. \par \par \uc2  Hariri also said on Wednesday he had no plans to devalue the Lebanese pound, which has been under increasing pressure as fears about the country's debt rise. \par \par \uc2  "We have no intention to devalue our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We think it is bad for the economy and we don't see any benefit for the Lebanese or the economy in general," Hariri said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pound trades in a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band of 1,510-1,514 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If we devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ..., the interest rates will jump and it will be followed by high inflation, which will hurt the Lebanese," he added. \par \par \uc2  Hariri said he was also concerned a devaluation would hurt the "credibility" of the country, and said it would be unfair to Lebanese who have kept their money in pounds. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020703dy7300yky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
