\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Bank of Beirut says buys Beirut Riyad. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lin Noueihed \par \uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2002\par 16:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, May 30 (Reuters) - Bank of Beirut, one of Lebanon's top 10 banks, has bought medium-sized Beirut Riyad Bank, officials of both banks said on Thursday, in a deal involving a share transfer but no cash. \par \par \uc2  "It is a done deal," said an official of Bank of Beirut. "Beirut Riyad is now fully owned by Bank of Beirut," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Talks on the deal were first reported by banking sources early this month. \par \par \uc2  An official at Beirut Riyad Bank, which has been asked by monetary authorities to improve its asset quality and raise capital, confirmed the news, saying that it would involve a transfer of shares. \par \par \uc2  "This had to happen or Beirut Riyad would have been forced to find a capital increase," he said, adding that the deal was signed on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Beirut Riyad, which has $760 million of assets, has not declared profits for three years because its earnings went to loan provisioning. \par \par \uc2  The bank, which is partly owned by Saudi investors, has 12 branches, including one in London. \par \par \uc2  Doubtful loans comprised 150 billion Lebanese pounds ($99 million) out of 487.3 billion pounds in total loans in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Beirut has been expanding under new management since the mid-1990s. In 1997, it bought Transorient Bank, a Lebanese bank. Emirates Bank International bought a stake in Bank of Beirut the same year. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Beirut's share on the Beirut Stock Exchange was unchanged at $7.81 on the news. \par \par \uc2  FORCED CONSOLIDATION \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the buyout will swell Bank of Beirut's branch network and is a logical move for the largely retail-based bank. \par \par \uc2  "It will boost the size of the bank and its market, which is what they're after," said one London-based analyst. "That makes sense given that organic growth is difficult in the current environment." \par \par \uc2  Habib Haddad, head of treasury at Beirut Riyad, said the buyout was "good news for both banks." \par \par \uc2  Faced with a retreating economy and rising bad loans, the monetary authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to force \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 67 banks to merge or raise their capital adequacy levels. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  routinely enourages banks to merge by extending them soft loans and the Bank of Beirut official confirmed that his bank would recieve such a loan, although he did not disclose the amount. \par \par \uc2  Bank profits have also been sliding as falling confidence in the government's ability to control a public debt equivalent to more than 165 percent of gross domestic product has put pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Lebanese banks have a high exposure to sovereign risk through large treasury bill holdings. \par \par \uc2  According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , doubtful loans rose to more than 12 percent of total loans at the end of last year compared to 10 percent in 2000. \par \par \uc2  "Given Beirut Riyad's problems, Bank of Beirut probably didn't have to splash out, but it's hard to tell if this is financially a good deal until we know what arrangements have been made for doubtful loans," said the analyst. "And whether more bad loans are lurking in the background." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020530dy5u00q38\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
