\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese pound remains steady, eyes on politics. \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2001\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Lebanon's pound was steady against the dollar in quiet trade this week, as the market looked ahead to a cabinet meeting on a set of politically charged government appointments, dealers said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  They said the pound was trading off the top of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band of 1,501-1,514 to the dollar at 1,513 during most of the week, with banks reversing the trend of much of the year and selling hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to defend the pound this year, as fears about the country's ability to service a public debt of $26 billion, equivalent to more than 160 percent of GDP, undermine confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the the pound, which local investors dump for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any sign of political strife, would take its cue next week from a Tuesday cabinet meeting at which a set of hotly contested administrative appointments will be made. \par \par \uc2  "The political climate is not that bad, but we still have Tuesday to overcome. If it goes smoothly, there are better days ahead of us," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major Lebanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Among the appointments the cabinet is set to discuss is the top job at the country's loss-making electricity company, which the government hopes to privatise to decrease its debt burden. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet meeting was scheduled after Lebanon's energy minister last week accused the head of the company - which collects only half its bills - of exacerbating power cuts in order to embarrass the government, and demanded he be sacked. \par \par \uc2  Sales of state assets including utilities and cellular phone operating licences are a key to Prime Minister Rafik al-Hariri's plan to slow the growth of the debt, which eats up the bulk of government revenue and chokes off growth. ($1=1,508 Lebanese pounds). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011207dxc700mzy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
