\par\par \b \uc2  Beirut banks eye fee business as loan, debt risk high. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Joseph Logan \par \uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2001\par 17:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, August 7 (Reuters) - Lebanon's banks will turn to retail services and other fee-earning business to help make up for conservative lending policies forced on them by the country's economic woes, bankers and analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Many of the country's leading banks reported stagnant or falling first-half profits, and said that macroeconomic pressures were forcing them to adopt conservative lending strategies to reduce the ratio of bad loans in their portfolio. \par \par \uc2  The Lebanese government, with debt of over $24 billion or about 155 percent of GDP, is facing mounting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while it turns to politically sensitive plans for fiscal reform and privatisation to cope with the debt and end several years of recession. \par \par \uc2  Local banks, meanwhile, are major holders of Lebanese pound-denominated debt, whose prices would drop with a devaluation. While reducing lending, they are seeking to draw income from retail services, such as processing payments of mobile phone and utility bills and offering Internet bank accounts and services, said a strategist for one bank. \par \par \uc2  "(The sector) is focusing on non-interest income and fee earning business, which is not affected as much by the economic sluggishness," said Marwan Barakat of Banque Audi , whose first half profits fell 9.5 percent year-on-year to $16.5 million. \par \par \uc2  Fellow leading bank Blom said cutting doubtful loans allowed it to increase first-half profits slightly from the previous year to $39.48 million, while Byblos Bank said conservative investment strategies helped bring its first-half profits down 7.8 percent year-on-year to $24 million. \par \par \uc2  An executive of one bank who asked not to be identified said the banking sector, traditionally a magnet for deposits from Lebanese living abroad and from Arab investors, was not crawling inside its shell completely but sought to manage its risk. \par \par \uc2  "Essentially it's your still-traditional approach of trying to lend as much as possible with good risk in the private sector," he said. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund last month warned Lebanese authorities that a slowing growth in the deposit base of local banks suggested a looming crisis in their ability to finance the government's borrowing needs. \par \par \uc2  Banks have extended their wariness of risk to debt denominated in pounds, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market heavily for months to prop up prices, the unidentified banker said. \par \par \uc2  "In the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheets you're seeing a big rise in T-bills (purchases), because the last few issues have not been fully subscribed. It indicates increasing awareness of the risk," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010807dx8700uq0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
