\par\par \b \uc2  Business as usual in Lebanon despite Mideast tensions. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Khaled Yacoub Oweis \par \uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2000\par 18:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under pressure, state finances are shaky and many do not rule out war, but the Lebanese flair for doing business continues unscathed. \par \par \uc2  In a country were hotels and factories went up in the midst of the 1975-1990 civil war and banks kept operating from blast-proof rooms, the violence the Middle East is currently witnessing pales by comparison. \par \par \uc2  "It's silly to say that people are not a bit edgy, but (property) business is going on almost as usual," said Raja Makarem, head of real estate consultancy Ramco. \par \par \uc2  Makarem said he was arranging a 20-year lease for a new shopping centre and the $10 million sale of a bank building in downtown Beirut. \par \par \uc2  "The mall deal is being finalised and the other buyer is still strongly interested in the bank building," said Makarem. \par \par \uc2  "A slowdown is inevitable, many people will wait and see, but Lebanon will not lose its resilience," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Israeli withdrawal from south Lebanon in May helped the real estate market recover from a sharp decline. There has been renewed interest in downtown Beirut, which is nearly monopolised by Solidere, the largest firm in the country. \par \par \uc2  Property was the main destination of foreign capital inflows after the end of the civil war. Some foreign investors are still reluctant to enter the market without peace between Lebanon and Israel. But others have no such qualms. Three Saudi businessmen are currently going ahead with a $70 million hotel on the Beirut seafront. \par \par \uc2  POSITIVE SPIN \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the exchange rate stable and has spent at least $160 million this week to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to dealers, who said depositors were switching their bank accounts to dollars. \par \par \uc2  But the newly changed dollars would be invested in Lebanese assets, said Habib Haddad, head of treasury at Bank Beirut Riyad. \par \par \uc2  He said the newly found dollar liquidity would be mostly invested in Lebanese eurobonds, which fell this week. The Republic's 2009 bond lost $1 and its spread rose by 40 basis points to 440. \par \par \uc2  "There was no significant selling of bonds and they dropped on low volume," Haddad said. \par \par \uc2  He said Lebanon has never defaulted on its sovereign debt, which are mostly held by local banks, and the country has managed to launch eurobonds in the bleakest of times, such as during the 1998 Asian crisis and during the recent tensions. \par \par \uc2  "In no time they found on Wednesday takers for a 250 million euros ($200 million) in an addition to their existing Eurobond," Haddad said. \par \par \uc2  On October 25 a new fund linked to Lebanese eurobonds and arranged by Bank of Beirut will be launched. Officials said a presentation to international investors is going ahead. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwac04dz0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
