\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Lebanese c.bank lowers GDP forecast. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miral Fahmy \par \uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1999\par 16:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Aug 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Riad Salameh on Tuesday forecast Lebanon's economy to grow in 1999 by around one percent and said there would be virtually no inflation due to the slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Salameh had previously expected gross domestic product to increase by between two and three percent this year and inflation to hover around the three percent mark. \par \par \uc2  "Based on the performance of the past seven months, we've changed our estimates (of GDP growth) so that it's closer to one percent. If activity gets better it might rise to two percent," he told Reuters by telephone. \par \par \uc2  According to government figures, GDP growth in 1998 was around three percent. \par \par \uc2  This year a contraction in investment and demand, estimated by Lebanon's leading Banque Audi at four percent and caused by the government's austerity measures, has driven the economy into recession. \par \par \uc2  The Banque Audi report said that a GDP growth of two percent required an increase in local and international demand to about $900 million, a figure the bank said would be near impossible to achieve in the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  Tax and customs increases, part of the government's 1999 budget and five-year economic reform plan, have also contributed to the slowdown, which Salameh said would partially drive inflation to near zero percent. \par \par \uc2  He had earlier forecast a 2.5 percentage point rise in inflation this year due to the government's new tax policy. \par \par \uc2  In April, the government raised duties on income, corporate profits, imports, alcohol, tobacco and gasoline. The taxes aim at increasing revenues by 2.5 percent from 1998 levels to 4,990 billion pounds ($3.31 billion) and curbing the budget deficit to 40.5 percent from 43.6 percent a year ago. \par \par \uc2  "Our firm monetary policy has also contributed to reducing inflation," Salameh added. "It's below our five percent ceiling and also below the three percent we forecast earlier." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages the pound by an official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band of 1,508 to 1,514 pound to the dollar. Salameh has said he would cause the pound to appreciate if inflation crossed the five percent mark. \par \par \uc2  But changing the exchange rate is currently far from Salameh's mind. Dollar supply in the Lebanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been plentiful over the past two months, during which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought around $450 million, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is anticipating lower rates and a stable pound which is why investors are shifting away from dollars. Also, they are locking in treasury-bill yields," he added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently slashed interest rates on Lebanese pound paper, mainly two-year treasury bills which now yield 15.36 percent compared to about 16 percent a few weeks ago. Yields on one-year treasury bills have also been falling steadily and are now at 14.77 percent. \par \par \uc2  $1 = 1,508 pounds ((Beirut newsroom +9611 745 306/8 fax 745 336 beirut.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8o01wut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
