\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese presidential choice caused rush to pound. \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 1998\par 13:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Oct 9 (Reuters) - Agreement on naming the head of the army as Lebanon's next president caused a rush into the Lebanese pound from U.S. dollars in the week ended on Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The heavy supply of dollars in the market this week made the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying about $135 million," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "This supply reflected the atmosphere of confidence in the near-term political future of the country," he said, echoing the feeling among other dealers feeling that a smooth transition to a new president would ensure stability. \par \par \uc2  Another dealer said: "The Turkish-Syrian crisis made some people hestitate in getting rid of their dollars, otherwise the supply of the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been much higher. \par \par \uc2  "Investors shifted towards Lebanese-pound denominated treasury bills," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid-offer official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band stood in the range of 1,502.50-1,515.50 Lebanese pounds per dollar against Friday's 1,503.00-1,516.00 - the usual appreciation set by the government. \par \par \uc2  Interbank trading stood on the lower edge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bid-offer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in the range of 1,502.40-1,502.60 from 1,503.00. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week Syria, the main foreign power broker in Lebanon, backed army commander Emile Lahoud as the next president and on Thursday the Lebanese cabinet asked parliament to amend the constitution to pave Lahoud's way to the presidency. \par \par \uc2  Syria keeps 35,000 troops in Lebanon. They originally entered to help to restore order during the 1975-1990 civil war. \par \par \uc2  Regional tension rose this week when Turkey threatened Syria over its alleged support for the Kurdistan Workers Party (PKK). \par \par \uc2  ((Beirut editorial (9611) 745308 fax 745336 \par \par \uc2  beirut.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dua901kqw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
