\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese pound edges down on week. \b0\par\par\uc2 186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Aug 14 (Reuter) - The Lebanese pound traded at the upper end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bid-offer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in the week ended on Thursday amid continued demand for U.S. dollars on the Beirut \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The inter-bank trading of the dollar was in a range of 1541.00-1541.50 Lebanese pounds against last week's range of 1,535.00-1,541.50, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bid-offer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band was 1,532.00-1,541.00 Lebanese pounds compared to last week's 1,532.50-1,541.50, continuing the official policy of a slight pound appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Investors who did not renew subscription in Lebanese-pound-denominated treasury bills shifted to dollars, said a dealer, who asked not to be identified. \par \par \uc2  "Demand for dollars was much higher than offer, which was very light because some matured treasury bills were not renewed," he added. \par \par \uc2  Banks and financial markets will close on Friday for the Assumption day holiday. \par \par \uc2  -- Beirut editorial (9611) 745307 745308 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8e01977\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
