\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Lebanon buys dollars - Governor. \b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, May 21 (Reuter) - The Bank of Lebanon has bought more than $900 million on the Beirut \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the start of 1997, Governor Riad Salameh said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a buyer of more than $900 million since the beginning of the year," Salameh told Arab bankers and financiers at a conference in Beirut. \par \par \uc2  The rate of dolarisation of bank deposits fell to 55 percent in April, he added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers told Reuters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had consistently bought dollars on the market this year. \par \par \uc2  "There have only been about 20 days on which the Bank of Lebanon did not take dollars from the market," Talal Abou Zaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Bank of Beirut and the Arab Countries, said. \par \par \uc2  "There are still people offering dollars and switching to Lebanese pounds, and I think there are also outside investors taking advantage of high interest rates on the Lebanese pound who are buying treasury bills," Abou Zaki said. \par \par \uc2  The Lebanese pound has continued its steady appreciation against the U.S. dollar during 1997. \par \par \uc2  It is currently at 1,542.5 to the dollar compared with 1,552 at the end of December 1996. \par \par \uc2  - Beirut editorial (9611) 345723 345740 602057 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5l05ah3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
