\par\par \b \uc2  Lebanese pound continues rise against dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, Jan 24 (Reuter) - The Lebanese pound continued to rise against the U.S. dollar in the week ended Friday in quiet trade, despite falling interest rates on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Lebanon\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its bid-offer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band to 1,545.5-1,555 pounds per dollar from 1,546-1,555.50, with the dollar continuing to trade at the bottom of the band throughout the week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "People are still interested in investing in the Lebanese pound despite the decrease in interest rates," Nabil Abou Jaoude, treasurer and chief dealer for Banque Libanaise pour le Commerce, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Trade on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was restricted to a very tight range of 20-25 piastres at the bid end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, Abou Jaoude said. All dollar offers were taken up by commercial banks, he added. \par \par \uc2  Yields on Lebanese pound-denominated treasury bills also continued to fall during the week. They now stand at 14.17 percent for three-month bills, 15.89 percent for six months, 16.38 percent for 12 months and 18.12 percent for 24-month bills. \par \par \uc2  The Lebanese Banks Association also reduced the pound prime lending rate last week to 18 percent from 24 percent to catch up with the downward trend in t-bill yields. \par \par \uc2  -Beirut editorial (961 1) 602057 345740 345723 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1o00ykj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw1